# Everything below is optional and independent. The console runs with the
# PingOne sign-in and Authenticate Agent buttons hidden until their block
# of variables is fully populated.

# --- OIDC (PingOne) — "Sign in with PingOne" -------------------------------
# Create a PingOne application (OIDC Web App) with:
#   Redirect URI:            http://localhost:8000/api/auth/callback
#   Post-logout redirect URI: http://localhost:5173
#   Grant type: Authorization Code, PKCE required (S256)
OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173
# Space-delimited scopes requested at sign-in. "openid" is required (it's
# what makes this OIDC rather than bare OAuth); add scopes here if the
# resulting access_token also needs to work as the `subject_token` for a
# broader token exchange than what profile/email alone would grant.
OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email

# --- Agent identity — "Approve Agent Action" (Client Credentials + RFC 8693) -
# Create a SECOND PingOne application (Worker/Service app) with:
#   Grant types: Client Credentials, Token Exchange
#   Token endpoint auth method: client_secret_basic
AGENT_CLIENT_ID=
AGENT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Space-delimited scope(s) for the agent's own actor token (step 1, Client
# Credentials) — what the agent can do as itself. Most PingOne worker apps
# require this explicitly, or the grant fails / returns an unusable token.
AGENT_SCOPES=
# The `aud` claim /api/invoke requires on a delegated token before it will
# use it (this is what makes token exchange mandatory for anything the agent
# does on the user's behalf, rather than decorative — inbound auth
# re-verifies signature/issuer/expiry/audience fresh on every call, like AWS
# AgentCore's inbound auth authorizer does, rather than trusting a session).
# Optional: falls back to AGENT_CLIENT_ID, which is what PingOne populates
# `aud` with by default for a token minted against a custom resource owned
# by the agent app.
#
# IMPORTANT: this requires the PingOne resource behind TODOS_READ_SCOPE /
# TODOS_WRITE_SCOPE to be configured to issue JWT (not opaque/reference)
# access tokens — inbound auth can only cryptographically verify a JWT.
AGENT_EXPECTED_AUDIENCE=
# Per-action delegation scopes (step 2, Token Exchange) — requested fresh,
# scoped to exactly the one action needed, the first time it's needed. Plain
# chat needs neither; asking the agent to view todos triggers exchange for
# TODOS_READ_SCOPE, asking it to add/complete one triggers a SEPARATE
# exchange for TODOS_WRITE_SCOPE (approving read doesn't grant write). Must
# be defined as valid scopes on the PingOne resource above, and must match
# task-agent/.env's copies of these two values exactly — it independently
# checks the granted scope on every tool call, not just token validity.
TODOS_READ_SCOPE=todos:read
TODOS_WRITE_SCOPE=todos:write

# --- Session cookie encryption (required if either block above is used) ---
# Any long random string; it's hashed to a 32-byte key for JWE A256GCM.
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- LangGraph agent --------------------------------------------------------
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
AGENT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# --- A2A: delegation to the Task Agent (see task-agent/) --------------------
# The Chat Agent forwards its own delegated token to this URL — see
# CLAUDE.md "Identity propagation across the A2A hop". task-agent/.env's
# OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL / AGENT_EXPECTED_AUDIENCE must match this service's.
TASK_AGENT_URL=http://localhost:9010

# --- Networking --------------------------------------------------------------
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
